4. app/models.py — Data Models
Defines the database entities (likely using SQLAlchemy or Pydantic):

python
# Typical models:
class Customer(Base):
    id, name, email, country, tax_exempt

class Invoice(Base):
    id, customer_id, items, status, created_at, total

class InvoiceItem(Base):
    invoice_id, description, amount, tax_rate, tax_amount
Responsibilities:

Defines tables (Customer, Invoice, InvoiceItem, etc.)
Captures relationships (one customer → many invoices)
Stores the data structure for persistency
Validates data types via Pydantic models (if FastAPI)
5. app/db.py — Database Connection
Manages the persistence layer:

python
# Typical contents:
from sqlalchemy import create_engine
from sqlalchemy.orm import sessionmaker

engine = create_engine(DATABASE_URL)      # e.g., postgres://...
SessionLocal = sessionmaker(bind=engine)

def get_db():
    db = SessionLocal()
    try:
        yield db
    finally:
        db.close()
Responsibilities:

Sets up connection to the database (PostgreSQL/MySQL/SQLite)
Creates the ORM engine and session factory
Provides dependency injection for database sessions
Possibly includes connection pooling and migration setup
